Network Troubleshooting and Monitoring

Overview

During my IT support experience at The Documented Warehouse, I provided first-line network troubleshooting for users and monitored network activity using 
Meraki.

My troubleshooting focused mainly on individual machines, while wider branch network issues were escalated to external network providers


Network Troubleshooting

* Checked that the network cable was connected correctly
* Checked how many users were affected by a network issue
* Determined whether the issue affected one user or multiple users
* Checked whether specific websites or services were affected
* Checked the machine's IP configuration and DHCP status
* Checked for APIPA addresses when a machine could not obtain a valid IP address
* Used ipconfig /release and ipconfig /renew to refresh the machine's DHCP address
* Restarted machines when required
* Checked whether the machine was still connected to the company domain
* Checked Ethernet adapter status when Windows displayed an Unknown Network
* Checked whether DHCP was enabled when the machine was expected to use dynamic addressing
* Flushed the DNS cache using ipconfig /flushdns when troubleshooting DNS-related connectivity issues
* Tested connectivity again after making changes

Unknown Network Troubleshooting

* Checked the network cable
* Opened Ethernet settings
* Checked the Ethernet adapter status
* Checked the DHCP configuration
* Checked whether the machine was connected to the company domain
* Restarted the machine when required
* Investigated possible APIPA, DNS, or proxy-related issues
* Flushed the DNS cache when the issue was DNS-related

DHCP and IP Troubleshooting

* Checked whether the machine received a valid IP address from DHCP
* Identified APIPA addresses when DHCP assignment failed
* Released and renewed the IP address using ipconfig commands
* Restarted the machine when required
* If the machine still could not obtain a valid IP address, the issue could be checked further at the DHCP level or escalated as required

Network Monitoring

* Used Meraki with read-only access for network monitoring
* Monitored office network traffic
* Viewed available network information to help investigate connectivity issues
* Used network information to help determine whether an issue was isolated to a machine or potentially affecting multiple users
* Did not make configuration changes through Meraki due to read-only access

Branch Network Issues

* Performed initial checks when multiple users or a branch experienced network problems
* Identified whether the issue was widespread or isolated to a specific machine
* Wider network infrastructure issues were handled externally by Echo and Tereco
* Continued troubleshooting directly when the issue was isolated to a specific machine

Wi-Fi Support

* Reset Wi-Fi connections when required
* Assisted users with connecting to the company's Wi-Fi
* Assisted users when the Wi-Fi credentials were changed as part of the company's three-month password update
* General Wi-Fi fault troubleshooting was not a primary responsibility

